A one-shot command-line run (`dafny verify`, `dafny resolve` and the other commands that resolve the program once) no longer keeps the parsed copy of the program, nor the parser and resolution caches meant for a next edit, once the program is resolved. After each module is reported, the solver checkers last set up for its Boogie programs drop them and their prover context, and visibility checks no longer accumulate an entry for every translation's scope. Each `--filter-position-batch` request no longer keeps the proof dependencies of earlier requests, so a batch's memory stays near that of one resolved program instead of growing with every request. None of these changes any output.
